          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Horace's Life</strong><br>\nQuintus Horatius Flaccus, known as Horace, was born on December 8, 65 BC, in Venusia, Italy. His father was a freed slave who worked hard to provide his son with a good education. Horace studied in Rome and then Athens, which was a center for learning in the ancient world. He started as a student of philosophy but found his true passion in writing poetry. His life took a dramatic turn when he joined the army and fought in the Battle of Philippi.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Early Career</strong><br>\nAfter the defeat of Brutus and Cassius's forces at Philippi, where Horace fought on the losing side, he returned to Rome. He started his career in the treasury department but soon focused on his writing. Horace's early poetry caught the attention of Maecenas, a prominent supporter of the arts, who introduced him to other writers and sponsored his work. This patronage allowed Horace to write full-time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Literary Work</strong><br>\nHorace's works include \"Satires,\" \"Epodes,\" \"Odes,\" and \"Epistles,\" all written in Latin. His satires poke fun at human vices and follies, while his epodes are short, sharp poems on various subjects. The odes are lyrical pieces that often celebrate love, friendship, and the simple pleasures of life. His \"Ars Poetica,\" or \"The Art of Poetry,\" provides insights into his views on literature and his poetic principles.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
