          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Influence of Greek Literature</strong><br>\nGreek literature greatly influenced Horace's writing. He admired Greek poets like Alcaeus and Sappho and adapted Greek meters to suit the Latin language. This blending of Greek forms with Roman themes and subjects was innovative at the time. He played a significant role in introducing Greek lyrical poetry forms into Latin poetry, which enriched Roman literature.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Themes in Horace's Work</strong><br>\nHorace's poetry often reflects on themes such as love, wine, nature, and philosophy. He is famous for his phrase \"carpe diem,\" which means \"seize the day,\" from his Odes. This reflects his belief in enjoying the present moment rather than worrying about the future. He also contemplated the role of fate and the nature of the gods in human life, urging readers to find a balance between desire and moderation.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Relationship with Augustus</strong><br>\nHorace had a complex relationship with Augustus, the first Roman Emperor. While he was initially skeptical of Augustus's rise to power, Horace was later commissioned to write propaganda in support of his regime. He composed odes that celebrated Augustus's achievements and contributed to the Emperor's efforts to restore traditional Roman values through his poetry.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
