          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Horace's Later Years</strong><br>\nIn his later years, Horace wrote less and reflected more on his life and times. His later poems show a shift from the playful to the philosophical, pondering the nature of life, aging, and mortality. He lived on his farm in the Sabine Hills, provided by Maecenas, enjoying the rural life which frequently inspired his writing.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Horace's Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nHorace died on November 27, 8 BC, and was laid to rest in Rome. His works have endured for centuries and remain significant in the study of classical literature. He is often quoted and his writings are still taught in schools and universities around the world. His advice on writing, philosophy, and lifestyle continues to influence readers and writers.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Remembering Horace</strong><br>\nToday, Horace is remembered as one of Rome's greatest poets. His mastery of the Latin language and his skillful blend of wit, insight, and lyricism have made his works timeless classics. The recurring themes in his writing, such as the importance of living a balanced life, resonate with readers even in modern times. Horace's impact on literature is a testament to his talent and the lasting power of his words.</p>\n"
              },
